// A RENAMED selective import of a std FUNCTION should call it under the new
// name, exactly as a renamed import of a user module's function does.
//
// `import std.task.{dump_tasks as dt}` type-checked — the rename registers the
// symbol under `dt` in the importing module's namespace — and then codegen looked
// the CALL up by the name at the call site and found nothing:
// `internal compiler error: Undefined function: dt`. The same rename over a user
// module (`import helper.{greet as hello}`) worked, and so did a renamed std
// TYPE (`import std.data.{Data as Bytes}`), which is what made this a std
// FUNCTION gap rather than a rename gap.
//
// FIXED (DF-187a): a function's namespace KEY doubles as its codegen name
// unless `mangled_name` overrides it, so the std re-registration now carries
// the original across as `mangled_name` — which is precisely what the
// user-module selective-import path had done all along, and why only std broke.
// On a COPY of the symbol, because `_expose` is handing out the shared, merged
// std symbol and renaming it in place would rename the definition out from
// under std. A type needed none of this: its codegen identity rides its symbol,
// not the key. Matrix: `import_rename_function_positions.saw`.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// saw tasks: none live
// ok

import std.task.{dump_tasks as dt}

func main() {
    dt()
    print("ok")
}
